"Two layers: a tradition is a long-lived stream (country, hip hop, Western art music), drawn as a soft band; a movement is a dated phase inside it (honky-tonk, boom bap, baroque), drawn as a dot with a bar for its active years. A new tradition branches off its parent as a wide, soft line. Zoom works like a map over one fixed layout: zoomed out, major movements are thick and named while the rest are pencil lines; zooming in brings in the dots and names of finer movements where they already sit. Dates are approximate first appearances; origin points are symbolic (a city or region where the style took shape). Each genre has one principal parent (closest style, not closest geography) and at most two others; these are judgment calls, as in any language family tree. The time axis is logarithmic, so recent centuries get more room. Bands group genres by sound. The order of the bands and each genre's row inside its band were set by an optimiser that minimises the total length of all parent–child lines (principal links weighted 1, others 0.4) without letting labels overlap. Time travel: each movement has peak years and the regions that widely loved it at its peak (plus regions it reached later). These are a first pass from general knowledge, coarse to ten world regions; colour fades after a peak ends, more slowly for more influential movements."
